description:
compile.ts lines 2046-2073: when a SymbolInstance inside a symbol timeline has buttonHandlers and the referenced symbol is type='button', compile.ts emits an inline DefineButton2 tag (with the per-instance handlers) and uses a fresh charId for placement. This is needed because button symbols can have unique per-placement handlers that override the base button handlers.

sprite.ts has NO equivalent logic in its instance placement block (around lines 463-526). It always uses the base charIdMap.get(displayObj.symbolId) charId and never checks for buttonHandlers or sym.symbolType === 'button'. This means button instances inside MovieClip/Graphic symbol timelines:
1. Never emit their per-instance button handlers in the SWF
2. Use the wrong charId (the base button's charId from the library symbol pass, which uses encodeDefineButton2 with the default handlers, not the instance's handlers)

Fix prescription:
In sprite.ts instance placement (isFirst block, around line 463), add after the hasClipActions / hasBlend checks:
  const instButtonHandlers = (displayObj as {buttonHandlers?: readonly ButtonHandler[]}).buttonHandlers;
  const sym = symbolById?.get(displayObj.symbolId); // need to pass symbolById into encodeDefineSprite
  if (instButtonHandlers && instButtonHandlers.length > 0 && sym?.symbolType === 'button') {
    const instCharId = nextCharId++;
    const instHoisted: Array<{tagType: number; body: Uint8Array}> = [];
    const buttonBody = encodeDefineButton2(instCharId, sym, doc, charIdMap, () => nextCharId++, instHoisted, instButtonHandlers, ...);
    for (const def of instHoisted) hoistedDefs.push(def);
    hoistedDefs.push({tagType: Tag.DefineButton2, body: buttonBody});
    // Use instCharId for placement below
  }

Note: encodeDefineSprite may need additional parameters (doc, symbolById, fontCharIdMap) already passed for the button encoding — check the existing function signature.

File: packages/swf/src/sprite.ts
